INSERT INTO blog_post_translations
  (post_id, locale, slug, title, excerpt, body_html, seo_title, seo_description)
VALUES (
  :post_id, 'en', $x$which-top-up-package-is-better$x$,
  $x$Small or large top-up package: which one pays off$x$,
  $x$Which top-up package is the better deal: we work out the price per unit on the real PUBG Mobile UC price list and show when a big package does not pay off.$x$,
$x$<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Compare packages by price per unit of currency, not by the total.</li>
<li>A bigger package is usually cheaper per unit, but not always: price lists have dips.</li>
<li>Sometimes two small packages cost less than one mid-sized one.</li>
<li>Buy the amount you will actually spend. Currency sitting on your balance saves nothing.</li>
</ul>
<p>The better package is not the one that is cheaper at checkout but the one with the lowest price per unit of currency — and only if you spend it. Below we do the maths on the real PUBG Mobile UC price list.</p>
<h2>How to work out the price per unit</h2>
<p>One formula:</p>
<p><strong>price per unit = package price in soʻm / amount of currency</strong></p>
<p>Work it out for the two or three packages you are considering and compare. The lower the number, the better the deal.</p>
<h2>Example: PUBG Mobile UC</h2>
<p><a href="/en/store/pubg-mobile">UC prices on YuPay</a>, a guide as of October 2026. The exact amount depends on the exchange rate and is shown before you pay.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Package</th>
<th>Price</th>
<th>Soʻm per UC</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>60 UC</td>
<td>≈ 11,900 soʻm</td>
<td>≈ 198</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>325 UC</td>
<td>≈ 60,400 soʻm</td>
<td>≈ 186</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>660 UC</td>
<td>≈ 120,400 soʻm</td>
<td>≈ 182</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>985 UC</td>
<td>≈ 183,100 soʻm</td>
<td>≈ 186</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1800 UC</td>
<td>≈ 307,800 soʻm</td>
<td>≈ 171</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3850 UC</td>
<td>≈ 594,000 soʻm</td>
<td>≈ 154</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5650 UC</td>
<td>≈ 907,200 soʻm</td>
<td>≈ 161</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>8100 UC</td>
<td>≈ 1,161,900 soʻm</td>
<td>≈ 143</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>11,950 UC</td>
<td>≈ 1,782,500 soʻm</td>
<td>≈ 149</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>What the table shows:</p>
<ul>
<li>The smallest package is the most expensive per unit: almost 200 soʻm per UC.</li>
<li>The best deal here is not the biggest package but 8100 UC.</li>
<li>985 UC costs more per unit than 660 UC. 5650 UC costs more than 3850 UC.</li>
</ul>
<h2>When two purchases cost less than one</h2>
<p>Because the list is uneven, it sometimes pays to build the amount from two packages.</p>
<ul>
<li>660 + 325 UC = 985 UC for ≈ 180,800 soʻm. A single 985 UC package is ≈ 183,100 soʻm.</li>
<li>3850 + 1800 UC = 5650 UC for ≈ 901,900 soʻm. A single 5650 UC package is ≈ 907,200 soʻm.</li>
</ul>
<p>The difference is small, but it is free. Before a big purchase, check whether neighbouring packages add up to the same amount for less.</p>
<h2>When a big package does not pay off</h2>
<p>A low price per unit becomes a saving only if you spend all of it. A big package makes no sense if:</p>
<ul>
<li>you need an amount for one specific item and the rest will just sit there;</li>
<li>you play irregularly and are not sure you will be back next month;</li>
<li>you are stocking up &quot;just in case&quot; with no goal in mind.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you buy once every few months for a specific item, take the package that covers it. If you spend currency every season on the pass and crates, a big package with a low price per unit pays off.</p>
<h2>First-purchase bonus</h2>
<p>Many games run one-off bonuses for the first purchase of a package in their own shop. If you have not used one yet, it is usually better per unit than any regular package. The terms differ from game to game, so check them in the game's shop before you do the maths.</p>
<h2>Other games</h2>
<p>The same rule works for Mobile Legends and Free Fire diamonds, Genshin Impact crystals and any other currency. The ratios between packages differ in every game, so there is no universal best size: do the maths inside your game. More on Genshin in <a href="/en/blog/genshin-impact-genesis-crystals">how to buy Genesis Crystals for less</a>, and on UC in <a href="/en/blog/how-to-get-uc-pubg-mobile">how to get UC in PUBG Mobile</a>.</p>
<h2>Paying in soʻm</h2>
<p>On YuPay you pay in soʻm with Click, Payme or Uzum. The total in soʻm is shown before you pay and exactly that is charged, so your price per unit does not drift at checkout. The currency is credited by player ID, no password needed. Where overpayment hides elsewhere is covered in <a href="/en/blog/hidden-fees-game-top-ups">hidden fees in game top-ups</a>.</p>
<p>Start simple: open the game's page, note the prices of two or three packages, work out the price per unit and take the one that fits how often you really play.</p>$x$,
  $x$Small or large top-up package: which one pays off$x$,
  $x$How to pick a top-up package: price per unit on the real PUBG Mobile UC list, when a big package is not worth it, and when two purchases cost less than one.$x$
);

